roger federer has announced his  retirement from tennis the 20-time grand  slam singles champion says the lava cup  in london later this month is going to  be his final event  federer posted this message on his  twitter this afternoon  i want to thank you all from the bottom  of my heart to everyone around the world  who has helped make the dreams of a  young swiss ball kid come true  finally to the game of tennis  i love you  and will never leave you  well i'm joined now by our sports  correspondent rob harris and rob what a  time this is for tennis it feels like  the end of an era serena williams  retiring and now roger federer  yeah the passing from the game the  departure of another great roger federer  announcing his retirement we can debate  who perhaps is the greatest of all time  and based on number of slams won novak  djokovic and rafa nadal have overtaken  roger federer now but  certainly by other barometers the most  weeks at number one in the world  rankings 237 weeks at number one he's  really set a mark in his graceful style  over the years and certainly his  longevity have established roger federer  as one of the greats the fact is he won  his first slam at the age of 21 2003  at wimbledon and he's gone on to win 20  slams but we've been hearing from him  today explaining just why he has decided  to  announce his retirement from next week  and this is what federer has had to say  in terms of setting out those reasons he  said  as many of you know the past three years  have presented me with challenges in the  form of injuries and surgeries i've  worked hard to return to full  competitive form but i also know my  body's capacities and limits and its  message to me lately has been clear  i am 41 years old i have played more  than 1500 matches over 24 years tennis  has treated me more generously than i  ever ever would have dreamt and now i  must recognize when it is time to end my  competitive career the laver cup next  week in london will be my final atp  event i will play more tennis in the  future of course but just not in grand  slams or on the tour now that event he  references next week it's a europe  versus the rest of the world event at  the o2 arena in london the final chance  it will seem to watch  roger federer playing a competitive  match the 41 year old swiss finally  deciding it's time to leave the court  and  the labour cup's just an indication of  perhaps some of his post-playing plans  because he actually helped to set up  that competition five years ago
 

